; SP16 Task 6 fixture - DAAD V3 database support. Compiled with -v3
; (tests\build-tests.ps1 -V3, and the differential runner under
; tests\parser\scripts\v3probe), so header byte 0 is 3 and every V3
; opcode below is live. Compiled WITHOUT -v3 this source does not
; build at all: DRF rejects '@' on a second parameter and rejects
; GETKEY outside V3.
;
; Everything the probes measure lands in flags, which is what the
; differential harness compares byte-for-byte between jDAAD and
; NextDAAD; the header line is for the human reading a transcript.
;
;   flag 100  LET 100 @101     -> 42, then PLUS 100 @102 -> 49
;   flag 104  LET @103 @101    -> 42 (BOTH indirections at once)
;   flag 106  flag 53 right after a DOALL that found an object
;   flag 107  flag 53 right after a DOALL that found nothing
;   flag 110  flag 59 after SETAT 0 1   (standard bank, set)
;   flag 111  flag 59 after SETAT 0 2   (standard bank, toggle back)
;   flag 112  flag 91 after SETAT 0 1   (alternative bank, base 91)
;   flag 113  flag 59 across the alternative-bank SETAT (must not move)
;   flag 114  HASAT 0  standard bank
;   flag 115  HASNAT 0 standard bank
;   flag 116  HASAT 0  alternative bank    <- see the divergence note
;   flag 117  the key GETKEY (= PAUSE 0 under V3) collected
;   flag 118  1 iff SYNONYM marked the process DONE
;   flag 120  flag 53 as PARSE left it - bit 4 (16) preposition before
;             noun1, bit 5 (32) unrecognised word after the verb
;
; KNOWN DIVERGENCE, flag 116. jDAAD's _HASAT (jdaad.js:3273) hardcodes
; base 59 and ignores flag 53 bit 1, while its _SETAT honours it. PCDAAD
; (condacts.pas:1207), msx2daad (_internal_hasat) and msx2daad's own
; executable spec (unitTests/src/tests_condacts_v3.c,
; test_HASAT_altflags / test_HASNAT_altflags) all honour it, so
; NextDAAD does too and the two legs are EXPECTED to disagree on flag
; 116, and on flag 116 ALONE: PRO 3's alternative-bank entry runs HASAT
; and nothing else, so flag 115 is written only by the standard-bank
; HASNAT, where both interpreters use base 59 and agree. Recorded, not
; a bug.
;
; SETAT STAND-INS - read before editing PRO 2. DRF 0.40 has no SETAT
; keyword: its condact table calls slot 124 "dumb", 0 parameters
; (SETAT only ever reaches a DDB by hand). Each SETAT below is
; therefore authored as a LET of the same arity, preceded by a unique
; "LET 250 n" tag, and the byte after that tag is patched from opcode
; 51 to opcode 124 once DRB has run. The tag is what makes each
; six-byte signature unique in the image, so the patcher can insist on
; exactly one match. NEVER run this DDB unpatched - the stand-ins would
; write flags 0 and 3 instead.
/CTL
_
/VOC
GO      10  verb
IND     40  verb
XM      41  verb
ATT     42  verb
KEY     43  verb
SYN     44  verb
DOA     45  verb
NORTH   2   noun
STONE   60  noun
IN      50  preposition
/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "I didn't understand."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "I can't do that."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 "Press any key."
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."
/MTX
/0 "SP16 T6 V3 PROBE#n"
/OTX
/0 "a stone"
/LTX
/0 "Arena."
/1 "The void."
/CON
/0
/1
/OBJ
/0    0   1   _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _   STONE _
/PRO 0
> _ _   CLS
        MESSAGE 0
; Flags 37 (max carried) and 52 (strength) are the one place the two
; interpreters differ before this fixture does anything: NextDAAD's
; eng_init_game seeds them 4 and 10, jDAAD leaves them 0. Pinning them
; here keeps that pre-existing, out-of-scope difference out of every
; single turn's diff so the V3 signal is the only thing left in it.
        LET 37 4
        LET 52 10
        MES "53="
        PRINT 120
        MES " I="
        PRINT 100
        MES "/"
        PRINT 104
        MES " D="
        PRINT 106
        MES "/"
        PRINT 107
        MES " S="
        PRINT 110
        MES "/"
        PRINT 111
        MES "/"
        PRINT 112
        MES "/"
        PRINT 113
        MES " H="
        PRINT 114
        MES "/"
        PRINT 115
        MES "/"
        PRINT 116
        MES " K="
        PRINT 117
        MES " Y="
        PRINT 118
        NEWLINE
> _ _   PARSE 0
        MES "NOPARSE#n"
; PARSE has run: flag 53's bits 4 and 5 are whatever this order's words
; made them, and the next PARSE will clear them again (they are
; per-logical-sentence, not per-line).
> _ _   COPYFF 53 120
        PROCESS 1
> _ _   REDO
/PRO 1
; ---- INDIR (122): '@' on a second parameter ----
; DRB emits INDIR immediately before its target and writes the flag
; number into BOTH INDIR's parameter and the target's placeholder byte:
; LET 100 @101 is 7A 65 33 64 65. The third entry combines it with the
; opcode's own $80 first-parameter bit (LET @103 @101 = 7A 65 B3 64 65),
; which means flags[flags[103]] = flags[101].
> IND _   LET 101 42
          LET 102 7
          LET 103 104
          LET 100 0
          LET 104 0
          LET 100 @101
          PLUS 100 @102
          LET @103 @101
          DONE
; ---- XMES (120): external text by 16-bit offset ----
> XM _    XMES "V3 XMES OK"
          DONE
; ---- SETAT (124) and HASAT/HASNAT (58/59), both attribute banks ----
> ATT _   PROCESS 2
          PROCESS 3
          DONE
; ---- PAUSE 0 == GETKEY under V3 ----
> KEY _   LET 117 0
          GETKEY
          COPYFF 60 117
          DONE
; ---- DOALL and flag 53 bit 0 ----
; Bit 0 is SET at DOALL entry and CLEARED on the first object found, so
; a DOALL that found something leaves it clear (flag 106) and one that
; found nothing leaves it set (flag 107).
> DOA _   LET 53 0
          PROCESS 5
          COPYFF 53 106
          LET 53 0
          PROCESS 6
          COPYFF 53 107
          DONE
; ---- SYNONYM done-semantics ----
; PRP019 V3-12: Z80 DAAD V2 marks DONE after SYNONYM, V3 does not. The
; probe is routed through a sub-process, which is the shape that reads
; the same on every interpreter: the PROCESS push clears the done state,
; so the LET above it cannot mask the answer and the ISDONE reports
; PRO 4's SYNONYM and nothing else. (It had to be written this way for
; the pre-SP18 engine, which read the last POPPED process instead of an
; accumulating flag - compliance report B20 - and it stays correct now
; that ISDONE accumulates.) Under V3 both interpreters must leave flag
; 118 at 0; flip this DDB's header byte 0 to 2 and the same bytecode
; must leave it at 1.
;
; The fall-through entry is headed SYN, not "_ _": a catch-all here
; would shadow every entry BELOW it, and this one has to be last
; because ISDONE failing is how the probe reports "not done". That cost
; one differential run to notice - the DOALL entry sat under a "_ _"
; and never ran.
> SYN _   LET 118 0
          PROCESS 4
          ISDONE
          LET 118 1
> SYN _   DONE
/PRO 2
; SETAT. See the stand-in note in this file's header before editing:
; each "LET 250 n" tags the LET on the NEXT line, which the -V3 staging
; step rewrites into opcode 124.
> _ _   LET 53 0
        LET 59 0
        LET 250 7
        LET 0 1
        COPYFF 59 110
        LET 250 8
        LET 0 2
        COPYFF 59 111
> _ _   LET 53 2
        LET 91 0
        LET 59 0
        LET 250 9
        LET 0 1
        COPYFF 91 112
        COPYFF 59 113
        LET 53 0
        DONE
/PRO 3
; HASAT / HASNAT. Standard bank first: attribute 0 lives in flag 59
; bit 0. Then the same attribute with flag 53 bit 1 set, where the bank
; moves to base 91 - flag 91 carries attributes 0-7 and flag 59 must be
; ignored entirely.
> _ _   LET 53 0
        LET 59 1
        LET 91 0
        LET 114 0
        LET 115 0
        LET 116 0
        HASAT 0
        LET 114 1
> _ _   HASNAT 0
        LET 115 1
> _ _   LET 53 2
        LET 59 0
        LET 91 1
        HASAT 0
        LET 116 1
> _ _   LET 53 0
        DONE
/PRO 4
; SYNONYM alone. Under V2 this level pops DONE, under V3 it does not.
; DRF resolves SYNONYM's parameters through the vocabulary, so this is
; the unit test's { _SYNONYM, 3, 4 } spelled with real words: it
; substitutes verb 10 (GO) and noun 60 (STONE) into flags 33/34.
> _ _   SYNONYM GO STONE
/PRO 5
; DOALL over a location that HAS an object (the stone is at 0).
> _ _   DOALL 0
        PLUS 105 1
/PRO 6
; DOALL over an empty location (1 holds no objects): cancels with NOTDONE + NEWTEXT and
; leaves flag 53 bit 0 set.
> _ _   DOALL 1
        PLUS 105 1
/END
